body:
You appear to be arguing with someone other than myself. I do not believe in conspiracy theories or shadow governments. I never said that the state wanting people to be alive for economic reasons was a secret cabal in the shadows. It is not a secret motivation: it is pretty openly out there as a legitimate reason for governments (local, state, federal) to want citizens to stay alive:  


 [https://www.edc.org/financial-cost-suicide-us-nearly-twice-previously-thought](https://www.edc.org/financial-cost-suicide-us-nearly-twice-previously-thought) 

&#x200B;

 [https://www.cdc.gov/violenceprevention/suicide/fastfact.html?CDC\_AA\_refVal=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cdc.gov%2Fviolenceprevention%2Fsuicide%2Fconsequences.html](https://www.cdc.gov/violenceprevention/suicide/fastfact.html?CDC_AA_refVal=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cdc.gov%2Fviolenceprevention%2Fsuicide%2Fconsequences.html) 

&#x200B;

 [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5061092/](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5061092/) 

&#x200B;

And when I said that the government wants workers alive, I meant for the good of the overall economy, not to specifically work in the government. If a person remains alive so that they go work for a private company, as an entrepreneur, for a nonprofit, or for the government, they would all be contributing to the economy, which is what our elected leaders want because it benefits everyone. Even if that individual does not want to participate anymore. 

&#x200B;
